 things that we really need  to address  well let me ask this  in uganda for example researchers have  developed and installed low-cost air  quality monitors in schools and other  areas to help address air pollution is  that a way to go  yes because uh you'll find like the  whole of africa i think they are  i think only seven countries have got  national air quality monitoring out of  54 countries that is really  that's a terrible situation that we  don't really know the extent of the  problem  so the the innovations that are coming  out of uganda they are going to help the  whole continent so that we can know what  is the situation at community level and  national level  the normal argument has been that it is  expensive to tackle air pollution but  you see nature-based mitigation  strategies are viable alternatives tell  us more about these alternatives  so technology is not always the silver  bullet to address all problems sometimes  it's the simplest like planting trees  planting flowers having trees on the  roofs you know when we create green  spaces we don't only beautify our cities  but we can also help clean the air so  those are kind of the ways that we can  use  okay now away from the role of  authorities what can someone like me do  to contribute to a cleaner air  so the first thing that i would really  recommend is we need to move away from  using wood and charcoal for heating and  cooking right try to use uh  clean up yours like biogas natural gas  right well guys you can actually  generate if you're from the village you  avoid agricultural waste you can  actually make biogas from that you can  also need to buy things more cheaper air  quality monitors i think they cost about  30 dollars then  again our emphasize plant trees grow  flowers in your house because sometimes  the beautiful things are the best  solutions  okay nice way to wrap that up edmond san  ganado environmental scientist thanks  for your time  now to breathe cleaner air we need less  pollution right as we've heard traffic  congestion is contributing to poor air  quality across africa but some people  are trying to make a change let's take  you to lagos in nigeria africa's most  populous city and certainly one of the  continents nightmares when it comes to  traffic meet the woman who's choosing to  ride two wheels rather than four  beating the rush hour traffic in lagos  drivers might be stuck in motorist  misery but cyclist asia fakaku zifi  wokolo zips past them on her way to work  she says the benefits of riding a  bicycle outweigh the dangers  one of the main reasons is  just getting to places a bit quicker  so there's a lot of traffic  there's no rail yet so what's the next  best option apart from walking or  running which will get you there quickly  and cycling  at least it's faster than being in  traffic  ziffy is one of a growing number of  bicycle enthusiasts in lagos  on weekends she rides for fun and  fitness with her club cyclotron  and as vice president she encourages  other younger riders to get on their  bikes  sifi is an inspiration to even men a lot  of guys cannot do what if he can do  she's got the legs he's good riding is  fun riding is enjoyable it's a good  workout it bonds with everything so i  recommend it for everyone who is not  currently riding  ziffy says cycling could be part of the  solution to lagos transport worlds  people are beginning to  embrace it the idea it's free one right  yo there's no cost of  we're not polluting the environment and  the health benefits  also as well so yes  there are more people  [Applause]  with zifi leading the way on her bicycle  she's setting an example  pushing for cycling as an alternative in  one of africa's busiest cities  you

